# ============================================================================
# SOFTWARE TESTING DONE RIGHT - a verdict with a reason
# Turing and Tau: seeing that something cannot be satisfied, seeing WHY, and
# repairing exactly there - the questions a test can never ask
# ============================================================================
#
# In the world of programs there are two things you can do with a rule set:
# run it, and test it. A run halts or it does not. A test passes or it fails.
# And a failing test says exactly one thing: THIS input, THIS run, went
# wrong. It can never say that NO input could succeed, and it can never say
# WHY - and since Rice (1953) that is not a gap in your tooling but a
# theorem: no nontrivial property of what a program computes is decidable.
# Whether ANY run of a program satisfies a requirement, whether ALL runs do,
# and which line of the program stands in the way - none of these questions
# has a general answer. So you observe failures one at a time, and you
# repair by guessing where the fault lives.
#
# Tau trades Turing completeness for decidability, and buys exactly those
# questions. A specification is a VALUE in an atomless Boolean algebra;
# deciding it is a normalization, not an execution (Tarski, 1949: the theory
# of these algebras is decidable, and the atomless one is complete). Two
# questions have exact answers:
#   sat   S     does ANY run satisfy S?      F is a proof that no run exists
#   valid S     does EVERY run satisfy S?    T is a theorem about all runs
# and the engine does not stop at the verdict. The normalized REMAINDER it
# prints on the way - the sentence with everything decidable already
# decided - names the values a rule set forces, the escapes it leaves open,
# and the freedoms nobody constrained. That remainder is the reason. This
# file is about the difference it makes: a verdict with a reason instead of
# a failure with a trace, and a repair aimed at the reason instead of a
# repair by trial.
#
# Ten parts. What a test is, in Tau's own terms (0). Unsatisfiable as a
# theorem, with its control (1). The remainder as the reason (2). Valid as a
# theorem, and its proof as a refused sat (3). The witness the engine hands
# you (4). The culprit, found by asking smaller questions (5). A targeted
# repair, then re-proved against the original intent (6). A rule about
# YESTERDAY that could never bite in a question - until the remainder named
# the freedom, and one aimed change made it real (7). Then the living
# counterpart of a test: a RUN, which is the witness over time, and which
# refuses to start where a program would crash later (8). And the finale:
# the same yesterday-rule, alive - in a run the past is real, and the
# verdict follows it step by step (9).
#
# Run with (minimal cost):
#   tau -q < testing_done_right.tau
# Verified against 0.7.0-alpha, build 1c1e58ae (2026-09-02 main).
#
# READING THE OUTPUT: "%N: ..." lines answer the sat/valid queries with T or
# F - consecutive IDENTICAL results share one %N slot (the REPL's memory
# de-duplicates against the last entry), so match the answers by ORDER, not
# by number (annotations say "Answer:"). A `sat always` query ALSO prints a
# line "Temporal normalization ... yielding the result: <formula>" - that
# formula is the REMAINDER this file keeps reading. In it, an apostrophe is
# Boolean complement: `o1[t]:tau' = 0` means "o1 is 1", `o1[t]:tau' != 0`
# means "o1 is not 1". Remainders are normal forms and may differ in
# spelling between builds; the T/F lines are the machine contract. The two
# `solve` queries print one `name := value` line per variable instead, and
# the two runs at the end print one `oN[k] := value` line per output and
# step - those values are the second half of the contract (EXPECTED-OUT).
# The console run reads every line after it as input, so it is the last
# thing in the file.
# ============================================================================

# EXPECTED-RESULTS: T F T F T T T F T F F T F T F T T F T F F F T T
# EXPECTED-OUT: 1 1 1 0 0 1 1 0

set charvar off

# ============================================================================
# PART 0: WHAT A TEST IS - in Tau's own terms
# ============================================================================
#
# Streams are named time series (o1, o2, ...); `o1[t]=1` says "o1 holds at
# every step"; `->` is implication, `&&` conjunction; `sat always S` asks
# whether any run obeys S. Take a rule set of two links: o1 forces o2, o2
# forces o3. A TEST of that rule set is a question in which every stream is
# pinned to a value - one concrete run:

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=1 && o3[t]=1)
# Answer: T - this run passes.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=0 && o3[t]=1)
# Answer: F - this run fails. Every test suite ever written is a list of
# lines like these two: one run at a time, with the input invented by you.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1)
# Answer: T - and this is the question no test can ask: leave o2 and o3
# FREE and ask whether any run exists at all. The engine answers for the
# whole space of inputs at once, without inventing one.

# ============================================================================
# PART 1: UNSATISFIABLE IS A THEOREM, NOT A FAILURE
# ============================================================================

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: F - o1 holds, o3 does not, and the two links connect them: no run
# at all, for any input, ever. Not "the inputs we tried fail" - "success is
# impossible", as a proof.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: T - the control: keep o3=0 but free o1, and runs exist again. Two
# questions bracket the fault between them - the fact o1=1 is what turns a
# satisfiable set into an impossible one.

# ============================================================================
# PART 2: THE REMAINDER IS THE REASON
# ============================================================================
#
# The engine did not try runs. It normalized the sentence - a quantifier
# elimination over the algebra - and what is left after everything decidable
# has been decided is printed as the remainder. Read it for the next query:

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1)
# Answer: T. The remainder line reads
#   o1[t]:tau' = 0 && o2[t]:tau' = 0 && o3[t]:tau' = 0
# i.e. o1, o2 and o3 are all 1: once o1 holds, the two links FORCE o2 and o3.
# That line is the reason in advance - it tells you, before you add any
# fact, that o3=0 can never be added to this set. Part 1 merely confirmed it.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: T. Pin the other end instead; the remainder reads
#   o3[t]:tau = 0 && o1[t]:tau' != 0 && o2[t]:tau' != 0
# "o3 is 0, and o1 and o2 are NOT 1" - the escape, named. Note the exact
# wording: not "o1 is 0" but "o1 is not 1". In an atomless algebra there is
# room between the two, and the engine states precisely what it knows.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o3[t]=0 && o2[t]=1)
# Answer: F - close the named escape (o2 must be 1) and the last door shuts.
# Two different facts kill the same rule set, and each time the remainder
# of the question WITHOUT the fatal fact had already said what that fact
# would have to contradict.

# ============================================================================
# PART 3: VALID IS A THEOREM - and its proof is a sat that is refused
# ============================================================================
#
# `valid always S` asks whether EVERY run satisfies S. A program can show
# you one run; a test can show you one counterexample; neither can show
# that there is no counterexample. Here is a theorem about all runs:

valid always (((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1)) -> (o1[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1))
# Answer: T - transitivity: whenever the two links hold, o1 forces o3. For
# every run, for every input, without exception. This is the shape of
# statement the Turing world cannot obtain about a program: not "it worked
# on our inputs" but "it cannot fail".

sat always (!(((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1)) -> (o1[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1)))
# Answer: F - and here is what "valid" IS: the negation of the statement
# has no run. A theorem is a satisfiability question, refused. One engine,
# one algebra, both questions.

valid always (((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1)) -> (o3[t]=1 -> o1[t]=1))
# Answer: F - the converse is not a theorem: the links do not run backwards.

sat always (!(((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1)) -> (o3[t]=1 -> o1[t]=1)))
# Answer: T - because a counterexample run exists (o3 holds, o1 does not).
# A test could have found that run by luck. Only a decision procedure can
# say, for the theorem above, that no such run exists.

# ============================================================================
# PART 4: THE WITNESS - the engine hands you the run
# ============================================================================
#
# A failing test needs you to invent the input. The engine goes the other
# way: ask it to SOLVE, and it prints an assignment that satisfies the
# sentence - the witness for "sat". (`solve` works on one-shot variables;
# `sbf` is the simplest Boolean type, and these two sentences are the
# Boolean core of the `sat always` questions above, one step at a time. The
# two queries below print `name := value` lines, not %N lines.)

solve (a:sbf = 1 -> b:sbf = 1) && (b:sbf = 1 -> c:sbf = 1) && a:sbf = 1
# Output: a := 1, b := 1, c := 1 - the forced run from part 2, made concrete.

solve (a:sbf = 1 -> b:sbf = 1) && (b:sbf = 1 -> c:sbf = 1) && c:sbf = 0
# Output: a := 0, b := 0, c := 0 - the escape from part 2, made concrete:
# with c pinned to 0 the only runs are the ones where nothing holds.

# ============================================================================
# PART 5: THE CULPRIT - ask smaller questions
# ============================================================================
#
# When a large rule set is unsatisfiable, drop clauses one at a time and ask
# again. A clause whose removal turns F into T is load-bearing - a culprit;
# one whose removal changes nothing is innocent. The innocent bystander
# here is the fourth rule, o4 -> o5.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && (o4[t]=1 -> o5[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: F - three rules, one fact, no run.

sat always ((o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && (o4[t]=1 -> o5[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: T - without the first link (o1 -> o2) runs exist: that link is a
# culprit.

sat always ((o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: F - without the o4 rule it is still impossible: o4 -> o5 is
# innocent. Three questions, no debugger, no trace - the fault is localized
# to a clause, and each step is a proof, not an observation.

# ============================================================================
# PART 6: A TARGETED REPAIR - and its proof
# ============================================================================
#
# Knowing the culprit, change it and nothing else. Dropping the fact would be
# surrender; dropping the rule would be too much. The aimed repair WEAKENS
# the culprit with a condition, so that the case at hand becomes an
# exception (o6 marks it). Then two more questions: is the repaired set
# satisfiable, and does the rule still do its old job everywhere else?

sat always (((o1[t]=1 && o6[t]=0) -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o1[t]=1 && o3[t]=0)
# Answer: T - "o1 forces o2, unless o6": with the exception available, the
# formerly fatal fact fits. The remainder reads o6[t]:tau != 0 && ... - the
# run must take the exception; the repair is visible in the reason.

valid always ((((o1[t]=1 && o6[t]=0) -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1) && o6[t]=0) -> (o1[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1))
# Answer: T - outside the exception the repaired rule still forces o3, on
# every run. This is the step the Turing world cannot offer: the repair is
# not "the failing test now passes" - it is a theorem about all the runs you
# did not test.

valid always ((((o1[t]=1 && o6[t]=0) -> o2[t]=1) && (o2[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1)) -> (o1[t]=1 -> o3[t]=1))
# Answer: F - the honest control: without restricting to the non-exception
# runs, the old guarantee no longer holds everywhere (the exceptional runs
# are exactly where it fails). A repair has a price, and the price is a
# decided sentence, not a hunch.

# ============================================================================
# PART 7: THE RULE ABOUT YESTERDAY - a freedom nobody would have tested for
# ============================================================================
#
# `o12[t-1]` is "o12 one step ago". State the rule "if o12 held yesterday,
# o13 is forbidden today", the fact "o12 does not hold today", and ask
# whether o13 is allowed today:

sat always ((o12[t-1]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=0 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: T - allowed. Now READ the remainder: it ends in
#   o12[t-1]:tau' != 0
# "o12 one step ago is not 1". Nothing in the sentence pins yesterday, so
# the engine tells you that yesterday is FREE - a run simply chooses a past
# in which o12 did not hold, and the rule never bites. A program with this
# rule would just run; a test with a history would pass or fail; no failure
# would ever say "your rule has no past".

sat always ((o12[t-1]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=1 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: F - with "o12 holds today" the rule DOES bite. Why? Under
# `always`, a fact about today is a fact about every step - yesterday
# included. The rule reads the present as if it were the past. Two verdicts,
# one remainder, and the diagnosis is complete: the past has no value of
# its own in this sentence.

sat always (o12[t]=0 && o12[t-1]=1)
# Answer: F - the proof that no added fact can fix this: "today 0, yesterday
# 1" is itself unsatisfiable under `always`, for the same reason.

# The aimed repair, at exactly what the remainder named: give yesterday a
# stream of its OWN. Read `o12[t-1]` as a separate stream (call it o9012)
# and pin THAT to what actually happened. Now the rule has a past, today
# keeps its own value, and the verdicts come out the way the rule was
# meant:

sat always ((o9012[t]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=0 && o9012[t]=1 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: F - yesterday o12 held (o9012=1), today it does not (o12=0), and
# o13 is forbidden: the rule bites on the past, not on the present.

sat always ((o9012[t]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=0 && o9012[t]=0 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: T - yesterday it did not hold, so today is free. Same rule, same
# today, different yesterday: the verdict follows the past, as it should.

sat always ((o9012[t]=1 -> o13[t]=0) && o12[t]=0 && o13[t]=1)
# Answer: T - and without a pinned yesterday the past is free again - now an
# honest "unknown", not a silent "same as today". The repair was one aimed
# change, and it was aimed because the remainder had said where the freedom
# was.

# ============================================================================
# PART 8: THE LIVING COUNTERPART - a run is the witness over time
# ============================================================================
#
# A test runs a program once. Tau can RUN a specification: outputs are chosen
# step by step so that the sentence holds at every step - the witness of
# part 4, now over time and printed live. Declare the three streams as
# console outputs and run the chain with o21 pinned:

o21:sbf := out console. o22:sbf := out console. o23:sbf := out console. run (o21[t]=1 -> o22[t]=1) && (o22[t]=1 -> o23[t]=1) && o21[t]=1
q
# Output: o21[0] := 1, o22[0] := 1, o23[0] := 1 - step 0 of a run that would
# go on forever; the `q` on the "continue?" prompt stops it. Nothing was
# invented: the values are the forced ones from the remainder of part 2,
# now produced by the machine that executes the sentence.

# And the run that a program would crash IN, Tau refuses to START:

o31:sbf := out console. run o31[t]=1 && o31[t]=0
# Output: (Error) Tau specification is unsat - before step 0. A program with
# a contradiction runs until it reaches it, possibly never in your tests;
# here the contradiction is a theorem, so there is no first step to take.
# The session goes on - the refusal is an answer, not a crash.

# ============================================================================
# PART 9: YESTERDAY, ALIVE - in a run the past is real
# ============================================================================
#
# Part 7 showed that in a QUESTION the rule "if yesterday, then not today"
# had no past, and how to give it one. In a RUN the past is real by
# construction: step t-1 has happened, and its value is on record. Feed the
# rule an input stream (i42) and watch the output (o43) follow yesterday's
# input, not today's. (Fresh names: a session binds a name and its type
# once, and o12/o13 are already the tau-typed streams of part 7.)
# The rule is completed with its mirror ("if not yesterday, then today") so
# that every step has a forced value and the witness is readable.
#
# Input per step: 1 0 0 1 1. Expected output:
#   o43[0] := 0   step 0 has no yesterday: the engine picks a value (here 0)
#   o43[1] := 0   yesterday's input was 1  -> forbidden today
#   o43[2] := 1   yesterday's input was 0  -> free today
#   o43[3] := 1   yesterday 0              -> free
#   o43[4] := 0   yesterday 1              -> forbidden
# The verdict follows the PAST, one step behind the input - exactly the
# behaviour the question in part 7 could not express until yesterday got a
# stream of its own. The run gives it that stream for free: the input a step
# ago IS a value, and the sentence reads it. The run reads every following
# line as input and ends at end of file - which is why it is the last thing
# in this file, and why the closing words come first.
#
# ============================================================================
# WHERE THIS LEADS
# ============================================================================
#
# Every step above was a question with a decided answer, and every answer
# carried its reason. Unsatisfiable was a theorem. The remainder was the
# reason. A theorem was a refused satisfiability. The engine handed over the
# witness, then ran it over time. Smaller questions found the culprit. The
# repair was aimed at it - and a validity check proved the repair kept the
# original intent everywhere else, and priced what it gave up. A run that
# could not exist did not start. None of these is available for a program:
# you can run it, test it and read its trace, but you cannot ask it whether
# ANY run exists, whether ALL do, or WHY not. That is Rice's theorem, not a
# missing feature. Tau pays for these questions with Turing completeness -
# and what it buys is a specification you can interrogate: every change to
# it is admitted or refused by a proof, with the reason attached.

i42:sbf := in console. o43:sbf := out console. run (i42[t-1]=1 -> o43[t]=0) && (i42[t-1]=0 -> o43[t]=1)
1
0
0
1
1
